1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,400
Your phone is the reason you have no identity.

2
00:00:02,740 --> 00:00:04,250
I came across this line in a

3
00:00:04,250 --> 00:00:06,790
Substack essay recently, and honestly,

4
00:00:06,790 --> 00:00:07,940
it made me pause.

5
00:00:08,030 --> 00:00:09,430
Because think about it,

6
00:00:09,430 --> 00:00:10,350
when was the last time you

7
00:00:10,350 --> 00:00:11,290
discovered something you

8
00:00:11,290 --> 00:00:12,880
genuinely liked without Instagram,

9
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:14,290
YouTube or algorithm

10
00:00:14,290 --> 00:00:15,310
recommending it to you?

11
00:00:15,640 --> 00:00:16,520
Look around.

12
00:00:16,540 --> 00:00:17,950
We are watching the same movies,

13
00:00:17,950 --> 00:00:19,330
listening to the same music,

14
00:00:19,330 --> 00:00:20,360
wearing similar clothes,

15
00:00:20,360 --> 00:00:22,350
visiting the same aesthetic cafés,

16
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:23,590
and sometimes even developing

17
00:00:23,590 --> 00:00:24,760
the same opinions.

18
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:25,340
And somehow,

19
00:00:25,340 --> 00:00:25,990
we still think we are

20
00:00:25,990 --> 00:00:27,700
expressing our individuality.

21
00:00:27,750 --> 00:00:29,770
But here is where it gets interesting.

22
00:00:30,030 --> 00:00:35,280
If you don't spend enough time getting to know yourself, you will end up absorbing everyone else's definition of you.

23
00:00:35,680 --> 00:00:36,700
And I think there is a much

24
00:00:36,700 --> 00:00:38,030
bigger idea behind this.

25
00:00:38,030 --> 00:00:39,000
There is a concept in

26
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:41,030
philosophy called the Culture Industry,

27
00:00:41,030 --> 00:00:43,260
introduced by Theodor Adorno

28
00:00:43,260 --> 00:00:44,500
and Max Horkheimer.

29
00:00:44,830 --> 00:00:46,210
They argued that mass-produced

30
00:00:46,210 --> 00:00:47,090
entertainment could make

31
00:00:47,090 --> 00:00:48,970
culture increasingly standardised.

32
00:00:49,120 --> 00:00:50,840
Now think about what algorithms do.

33
00:00:50,920 --> 00:00:51,920
You like something once,

34
00:00:51,920 --> 00:00:52,510
and suddenly you are

35
00:00:52,510 --> 00:00:53,940
surrounded by more of the same.

36
00:00:53,940 --> 00:00:55,540
Your preferences become predictable,

37
00:00:55,540 --> 00:00:57,200
your feed becomes familiar, and slowly,

38
00:00:57,200 --> 00:00:58,380
discovering something outside

39
00:00:58,380 --> 00:01:00,030
the bubble starts feeling unusual.

40
00:01:00,420 --> 00:01:01,320
But can we really blame

41
00:01:01,320 --> 00:01:02,740
algorithms or capitalism?

42
00:01:02,740 --> 00:01:03,190
After all,

43
00:01:03,190 --> 00:01:05,270
we also choose to consume what they offer.

44
00:01:05,410 --> 00:01:06,690
Maybe the real problem is that

45
00:01:06,690 --> 00:01:07,610
we have lost the habit of

46
00:01:07,610 --> 00:01:08,760
exploring without being told

47
00:01:08,760 --> 00:01:09,720
what to explore.

48
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:11,340
And perhaps the most important

49
00:01:11,340 --> 00:01:12,400
question isn't whether the

50
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:14,220
internet is changing our identity.

51
00:01:14,270 --> 00:01:15,100
It's this:

52
00:01:15,180 --> 00:01:16,080
if you stepped away from

53
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:17,590
everyone else's opinions for a while,

54
00:01:17,590 --> 00:01:18,620
would you still

55
00:01:18,900 --> 00:01:20,710
know what you actually like?
